WEBVTT

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:05.300
A Sequence diagram explains who interacts with whom and in
what order.

00:00:05.420 --> 00:00:10.745
It is useful when a component picture does not tell you
how a request actually proceeds.

00:00:10.865 --> 00:00:16.490
We will model a customer submitting a request through a
web app and API to a database.

00:00:16.610 --> 00:00:23.660
You will create participants and messages, inspect
lifecycle and fragment controls, and review an error
branch.

00:00:23.780 --> 00:00:32.280
The goal is a readable explanation of behavior, not a
drawing that implies more concurrency or timing precision
than the model contains.

00:00:35.800 --> 00:00:40.850
Participants represent the actors and systems taking part
in the interaction.

00:00:40.970 --> 00:00:44.945
Use Insert and Participant to add a support service.

00:00:45.065 --> 00:00:53.265
The kind can represent an actor, boundary, control,
entity, database, collection or queue, depending on what
you need to communicate.

00:00:53.385 --> 00:00:59.035
Use an alias when a readable display name would be
cumbersome in repeated message statements.

00:00:59.155 --> 00:01:06.480
Inspect the created declaration and keep naming consistent
with the other diagrams in the customer portal document.

00:01:10.000 --> 00:01:13.950
Select the API participant to open its inspector.

00:01:14.070 --> 00:01:18.970
Participant settings include identity and presentation
choices.

00:01:19.090 --> 00:01:27.390
Review aliases, stereotypes, colors and ordering without
assuming that the visible display name is the only
reference used elsewhere.

00:01:27.510 --> 00:01:34.935
This inspector applies related changes together with
Apply, so it differs from a field that commits when focus
leaves.

00:01:35.055 --> 00:01:39.205
Make an intentional change and check the messages
afterward.

00:01:39.325 --> 00:01:45.275
Close or cancel an unneeded draft rather than leaving an
unexplained pending edit.

00:01:48.800 --> 00:01:53.400
A message identifies sender, receiver and the interaction
text.

00:01:53.520 --> 00:02:00.270
Use the message creation screen to review the participant
pickers, message kinds and advanced modifiers.

00:02:00.390 --> 00:02:07.765
A request and a return are different parts of the story;
arrow shape and direction should express your meaning
consistently.

00:02:07.885 --> 00:02:15.360
Existing and newly introduced names need to be deliberate
so that a typo does not create an unintended participant.

00:02:15.480 --> 00:02:21.180
Inspect a message's source statement before drawing
conclusions from its position alone.

00:02:24.700 --> 00:02:29.775
Activation represents a period of activity on a
participant's lifeline.

00:02:29.895 --> 00:02:36.020
It is useful when you want to distinguish being present in
an interaction from actively handling a call.

00:02:36.140 --> 00:02:42.765
Paste the supplied extended example and follow the
activation of Web and API through the request.

00:02:42.885 --> 00:02:46.185
Deactivation ends those active regions.

00:02:46.305 --> 00:02:58.780
Creation, destruction, return shortcuts and message
modifiers have more specialized meanings; use a small
reference example to check the syntax before combining
them into a complicated interaction.

00:03:02.300 --> 00:03:06.300
Follow the worker lifecycle in this small source example.

00:03:06.420 --> 00:03:12.970
Creation introduces Worker at its first received message,
while activation marks its active work.

00:03:13.090 --> 00:03:18.815
The return statement completes the most recent active call
and produces the receipt.

00:03:18.935 --> 00:03:22.685
Destruction ends the worker lifeline after release.

00:03:22.805 --> 00:03:29.180
Read these statements alongside the rendered interaction
before combining them into a larger model.

00:03:32.700 --> 00:03:37.875
The combined fragment in this example separates valid and
invalid requests.

00:03:37.995 --> 00:03:42.795
Read each branch condition and follow the messages within
that branch.

00:03:42.915 --> 00:03:50.115
Other fragment families include optional behavior, loops,
parallel work, breaks, critical regions and grouping.

00:03:50.235 --> 00:03:54.610
Choose the construct for its behavioral meaning, not just
its border.

00:03:54.730 --> 00:04:00.980
The structure inspector and source show where the fragment
begins, where branches change and where it ends.

00:04:01.100 --> 00:04:06.325
Keep nested fragments small enough that a reader can
follow one path without guessing.

00:04:09.833 --> 00:04:13.833
An optional block represents a conditional interaction.

00:04:13.953 --> 00:04:18.503
A loop represents repeated interaction under its stated
condition.

00:04:18.623 --> 00:04:23.898
Parallel branches describe concurrent work, rather than an
alternative choice.

00:04:24.018 --> 00:04:28.793
A break identifies an interaction that terminates its
enclosing path.

00:04:28.913 --> 00:04:35.113
A critical region identifies work that must be considered
indivisible in this interaction model.

00:04:35.233 --> 00:04:42.783
A named group organizes related messages; these blocks are
separate teaching examples supplied with the practice
files.

00:04:46.300 --> 00:04:51.300
Notes explain assumptions or context that would make a
message label too long.

00:04:51.420 --> 00:04:55.395
A reference can point to a separately described
interaction.

00:04:55.515 --> 00:05:03.115
Separators, delays, spacing, duration arrows, page breaks
and numbering controls each affect how the story is read.

00:05:03.235 --> 00:05:08.085
The example numbers its messages and places a note beside
Web.

00:05:08.205 --> 00:05:14.305
Inspect these facilities in Insert, then choose only the
ones that improve the explanation.

00:05:14.425 --> 00:05:21.225
Adding many presentation devices cannot repair an unclear
interaction or a missing branch condition.

00:05:24.733 --> 00:05:28.908
A reference names a separately described interaction
contract.

00:05:29.028 --> 00:05:33.578
Separators and automatic numbering organize the message
sequence.

00:05:33.698 --> 00:05:40.773
A delay and explicit spacing affect how the interaction is
read, while notes retain explanatory context.

00:05:40.893 --> 00:05:47.243
Page breaks remain explicit source statements; check the
page handling of your intended export.

00:05:47.363 --> 00:05:53.638
Duration anchors use the Teoz layout to mark a measured
span between two points in the interaction.

00:05:53.758 --> 00:06:01.983
Inspect the source and actual preview for these
constructs, and use Issues when a supported source feature
has a visual-editing limitation.

00:06:05.500 --> 00:06:09.775
Participant order and message placement affect
readability.

00:06:09.895 --> 00:06:16.345
Use the diagram's supported dragging or reordering
controls and inspect the resulting source order.

00:06:16.465 --> 00:06:24.390
Open Diagram settings to compare alignment, wrapping,
spacing, boxes and layout choices such as Teoz where
supported.

00:06:24.510 --> 00:06:30.160
These settings should make the interaction easier to
follow without changing its intended meaning.

00:06:30.280 --> 00:06:41.580
If a valid language construct has no dedicated visual
editor, keep it in source and review the rendered result
rather than promising that every syntax variant has its
own form.

00:06:45.100 --> 00:06:48.575
Open the reorder panel for this small interaction.

00:06:48.695 --> 00:06:55.470
Move API before Web in the participant list and inspect
the declaration order and lifeline layout.

00:06:55.590 --> 00:06:59.715
Move Check attachments before Check draft in the message
list.

00:06:59.835 --> 00:07:06.385
Message order changes the interaction being described, so
check the authored statements and drawing together.

00:07:06.505 --> 00:07:11.080
Undo both reorder operations to recover the original
sequence.

00:07:11.200 --> 00:07:18.200
Finally change the diagram title through its presentation
settings and verify the committed title in source.

00:07:21.700 --> 00:07:26.000
Use modifier selection to choose Web and API together.

00:07:26.120 --> 00:07:29.595
Inspect the bulk panel and apply a shared style.

00:07:29.715 --> 00:07:39.415
This is useful when several participants belong to one
category, but a box or a meaningful label can communicate
that grouping more directly than color alone.

00:07:39.535 --> 00:07:46.535
Copy and duplicate only a deliberate selection, then
inspect the messages and identifiers in the result.

00:07:46.655 --> 00:07:51.855
The common source history remains the place to reconsider
a bulk operation.

00:07:55.367 --> 00:08:01.092
Pause and build the successful request interaction, then
add a validation-error branch.

00:08:01.212 --> 00:08:06.162
Include one activation and one note that explains a real
assumption.

00:08:06.282 --> 00:08:13.732
Inspect a participant and a message through their visual
controls, and apply one shared style to a verified
selection.

00:08:13.852 --> 00:08:21.252
Your success check is that someone can trace the
successful and unsuccessful paths and identify who
performs each step.

00:08:21.372 --> 00:08:27.297
Use the supplied source examples to check specialized
arrow and lifecycle syntax separately.

00:08:30.800 --> 00:08:40.700
Use participants for the actors and systems, messages for
their interactions, and lifecycle or fragment constructs
only when they express an important distinction.

00:08:40.820 --> 00:08:45.895
Review identity, endpoints and branch conditions before
tuning appearance.

00:08:46.015 --> 00:08:50.540
Keep the source available for precise syntax and reference
examples.

00:08:50.660 --> 00:08:59.210
We will reuse this same portal behavior when comparing
capabilities, types, architecture and workflows in the
following modeling lessons.
